= 1. Modes

Vim is a modal editor. Understanding modes is crucial.

- Normal Mode: For navigation and manipulation. Default mode (`Esc`).
- Insert Mode: For typing text (`i`, `a`, `o`, etc.).
- Visual Mode: For selecting text blocks (`v`, `V`, `Ctrl-v`).
- Command-line Mode: For executing complex commands (`:`).

= 2. Basic Navigation (Normal Mode)

- `h`, `j`, `k`, `l`: Left, Down, Up, Right.
- `w`, `b`: Move forward/backward by word.
- `0`, `$`: Move to the beginning/end of the line.
- `gg`, `G`: Move to the beginning/end of the file.
- `Ctrl-u`, `Ctrl-d`: Scroll half-page up/down.

= 3. Editing Commands

- `i`: Insert before cursor.
- `a`: Append after cursor.
- `o`, `O`: Open new line below/above.
- `x`: Delete character under cursor.
- `dd`: Delete (cut) current line.
- `yy`: Yank (copy) current line.
- `p`: Paste after cursor.
- `u`, `Ctrl-r`: Undo, Redo.

= 4. Search and Replace

- `/pattern`: Search forward.
- `?pattern`: Search backward.
- `n`, `N`: Move to next/previous match.
- `:%s/old/new/g`: Replace all occurrences in the file.
- `:%s/old/new/gc`: Replace with confirmation.

= 5. Visual Mode Actions

- `v`: Character-wise selection.
- `V`: Line-wise selection.
- `Ctrl-v`: Block-wise selection (great for column editing).
- `d`, `y`: Delete/Yank selected text.

= 6. Windows and Buffers

- `:e filename`: Open a file in a new buffer.
- `:ls`: List all active buffers.
- `:sp`, `:vsp`: Split window horizontally/vertically.
- `Ctrl-w` + `h, j, k, l`: Move between windows.

= 7. Exit and Save

- `:w`: Save (write).
- `:q`: Quit.
- `:wq` or `:x`: Save and quit.
- `:q!`: Quit without saving.


= Pro Tips

== Commands with Numbers
Most commands can be prefixed with a number.
- `3dd`: Delete 3 lines.
- `10j`: Move down 10 lines.

== Text Objects
Powerful manipulation based on syntax structure.
- `diw`: Delete inside word.
- `ci"`: Change inside matching double quotes.
- `da(`: Delete a parenthetical block including brackets.

== Markers and Jumps
- `m[a-z]`: Create a mark.
- `'[a-z]`: Jump to the mark.
- `Ctrl-o`, `Ctrl-i`: Jump back/forward in the jump history.
